Hi Tom,

One other note is that in the upcoming release (which should be done
in a couple weeks), we're fixing a bug affecting use of the auxiliary
particle filter with saveAll=FALSE.
If you're sampling the latent nodes in PMCMC using the auxiliary
filter, you should be fine, because in that case we set saveAll=TRUE.

But on the off-chance you didn't want to monitor the latent nodes (in
which case we set saveAll=FALSE), please update
to version 0.9.0 when it comes out (or in the interim, install nimble
off our 'devel' branch on github).

-chris

On Sun, Nov 17, 2019 at 10:19 AM Daniel Turek <db...@williams.edu> wrote:
>
> For recording MCMC samples, you'll need to add a monitor for "x" using the MCMC configuration object:
>
> timeConf$addMonitors("x")
>
> The options you tried, saveAll = TRUE, will control how the nested particle filter algorithm (inside the RW_PF_block MCMC sampler) will operate, but that's located much deeper within the MCMC that you're using.  It sounds like you just want the MCMC algorithm to monitor "x".
>
>
>
> On Sun, Nov 17, 2019 at 6:56 PM Tom Hull <beta...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> Hi
>>
>> Pretty sure I'm missing something obvious, but how do I extract the samples for the latent states when using the `RW_PF_block` MCMC sampler?
>>
>> e.g.
>> timeModelCode <- nimbleCode({
>>   x[1] ~ dnorm(mu_0, 1)
>>   y[1] ~ dnorm(x[1], 1)
>>   for(i in 2:t){
>>     x[i] ~ dnorm(x[i-1] + c, pro_error)
>>     y[i] ~ dnorm(x[i], obs_error)
>>   }
>>
>>   pro_error ~ dnorm(0, 1)
>>   obs_error ~ dnorm(0, 1)
>>   c ~ dnorm(1,1)
>>   mu_0 ~ dnorm(0, 1)
>> })
>>
>> # simulate some data
>> seed = 159
>> t <- 25
>> mu_0 <- -10
>> pro_error <- 0.01
>> obs_error <- 0.5
